---
import LegalPage from '../components/LegalPage.astro';
import { LINKS, SITE } from '../data/site';

const ga = Boolean(SITE.gaId);
---

<LegalPage
  title="Cookie Policy"
  description={
    ga
      ? 'The NeuroGIDAS website uses Google Analytics cookies to count visits. Here is what that means and how to turn it off.'
      : 'The NeuroGIDAS website does not use cookies or tracking. Here is what that means for you.'
  }
  updated={ga ? 'October 21, 2026' : 'October 20, 2026'}
  summary={
    ga
      ? [
          'This website uses Google Analytics, which sets a few cookies so we can count visits.',
          'We only use it to see things like how many people visit and which pages they read.',
          'No ads, and we never sell or share the data.',
          'If your browser sends Global Privacy Control or Do Not Track, analytics does not load at all.',
          'Sites we link to, like Instagram or GroupMe, may use their own cookies after you leave our site.',
        ]
      : [
          'This website does not set any cookies.',
          'It does not use local storage, tracking pixels, or analytics.',
          'That is why there is no cookie banner.',
          'Sites we link to, like Instagram or GroupMe, may use their own cookies after you leave our site.',
        ]
  }
>
  <h2>What cookies are</h2>
  <p>
    Cookies are small files a website can save in your browser. Sites often use them to remember logins, save settings, or track
    what you do for advertising and analytics.
  </p>

  {
    ga ? (
      <>
        <h2>Cookies on this site</h2>
        <p>
          We use <a href="https://marketingplatform.google.com/about/analytics/" rel="noopener">Google Analytics</a> to see how
          many people visit the site, which pages they read, and roughly where they come from (like Instagram or a search). It
          sets these cookies:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>_ga</strong> stores a random number so Google Analytics can tell if the same browser comes back. It lasts up
            to 2 years.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>_ga_</strong> followed by letters and numbers keeps track of your current visit. It also lasts up to 2 years.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          These cookies don’t contain your name, email, or anything you typed. We turned off Google’s advertising features, so
          the data is not used for ads. We only look at totals, like “40 people visited this week.”
        </p>
        <p>
          The other small scripts on the site run inside your own browser to do things like open the menu on phones, show which
          meeting is next, and let you tap through the schedule. They don’t save anything about you.
        </p>

        <h2>How to turn it off</h2>
        <p>Any one of these works:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Turn on Global Privacy Control or Do Not Track in your browser. When either is on, this site never loads Google
            Analytics.
          </li>
          <li>Block or delete cookies for this site in your browser settings.</li>
          <li>
            Install Google’s{' '}
            <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" rel="noopener">
              Google Analytics opt-out add-on
            </a>
            .
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>The site works exactly the same either way.</p>
      </>
    ) : (
      <>
        <h2>Cookies on this site</h2>
        <p>
          We don’t use any. This site does not set cookies, and it doesn’t use similar tools like local storage, session storage,
          tracking pixels, or fingerprinting. We also don’t load any outside scripts, ads, or embedded social media posts that
          could set cookies for other companies.
        </p>
        <p>
          The only small scripts on the site run inside your own browser to do things like open the menu on phones, show which
          meeting is next, and let you tap through the schedule. They don’t save anything about you.
        </p>

        <h2>Why there’s no cookie banner</h2>
        <p>
          Cookie banners ask for your permission to store cookies. Since this site doesn’t store any, there is nothing to ask
          permission for.
        </p>
      </>
    )
  }

  <h2>Other websites</h2>
  <p>
    When you click a link to another site, such as GroupMe, Instagram, Google Calendar, or a research database, that site may use
    its own cookies. Their cookie and privacy policies apply there. You can control or delete cookies from other sites in your
    browser settings.
  </p>

  <h2>If this changes</h2>
  <p>
    If we add or change anything that uses cookies, we will update this page before we do it, change the date at the top, and
    ask for your consent where the law requires it.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions? Email <a href={`mailto:${LINKS.email}`}>{LINKS.email}</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
